From 0362ad4c6cc44c3198bca0c5daa4bcc31cfec705 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:18:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: frame start margin recovers 1 ms per 60 frames; per-draw
 GPU timing of the costliest pass

Two changes from the b61 log with VKD3D fex8 (Jesse, 2026-10-24):

1. Frame start margin shrinks by 1 ms per 60 frames (2 s) without a late
   frame, was 150 (5 s). Each fight pushed it to 8-12 ms and it then
   stayed above 5 ms for 30-45 s; medium scenes (GPU 60-90% busy) took
   53-66 ms from frame start to screen in those seconds against ~45 ms
   with a margin of 3-5 ms (105 of 190 medium seconds were affected).
   Late frames came at changes of scene whatever the margin was (none in
   85 s of medium scenes at a margin of 3-5 ms). Replaying the run's
   margin steps with the faster decay gives ~45.7 ms for medium scenes
   (51.4 measured).

2. Draw timing in the pass meter (VKD3D_FEX_PASSES=1, off with
   VKD3D_FEX_DRAWS=0). Fights are GPU-bound at 26-29 FPS and the main
   pass costs ~11-20 us of GPU time per draw while shading ~1.2 fragments
   per pixel. Once a second the meter thread arms one sample for the
   kind of pass with the most GPU time (50+ draws per pass); the first
   command list to begin such a pass takes it and writes a top-of-pipe
   timestamp before every draw (up to 2040), one before
   vkCmdBeginRendering and an all-commands one after vkCmdEndRendering.
   The report shows time per draw (to the next draw's timestamp) as a
   histogram, draws after a pipeline change vs not, small vs large draws,
   the slowest draws, and the 4 costliest pipelines with the driver's
   shader statistics (VK_KHR_pipeline_executable_properties, enabled when
   the driver has it: waves per core, instructions, registers, texture and
   memory instructions, spills, sync stalls; the driver's statistic names
   are listed once). Every 4th sample instead writes all-commands
   timestamps around every 8th draw (the driver waits for the GPU to go
   idle first) and compares those draws alone with the same pipelines'
   time per draw in the last normal sample: about the same means the GPU
   doesn't overlap draws. The pool is reset on the host between samples
   (hostQueryReset), the sample's pipeline states hold an internal
   reference until the thread has read their statistics, and a sample in
   a list that is reset or freed without submitting is dropped. The
   '#' notes now go out with the first 10 reports only and the pass-end
   line is left out of reports that carry a draw sample, so both fit in
   the 4 KB report.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
